  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x>0,\\,y>0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$",
  "worked_solution": "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS=(0,\\infty )\\times (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)={e}^{-x}$ and $h(y)={e}^{-y}.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$",
